# Migration Plan Template

Use for a transition requiring a durable staged plan. Planning and recording approval do not authorize additional execution or external targets.

## Transition and scope

State the current/target versions or formats, affected content/consumers, authorized scope, applicable revision, and known constraints. Mark unknown inventory and volume explicitly.

## Invariants and coexistence

Describe what must survive transformation and which readers/writers can coexist. Identify any consistency cost or old/new incompatibility; do not assume dual writes or a compatibility layer is needed.

## Stages and stop conditions

| Stage/action | Prerequisites | Acceptance evidence | Stop condition | Responsible party if known |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |

Include preparation, representative trial, cutover, and retirement where relevant. Thresholds must come from requirements or be marked proposed. Record executed actions separately from planned actions.

## Recovery limits

Identify lossy/irreversible steps and the point after which old-version rollback is unsafe. Separate rollback, tested restore, forward repair, and reconciliation. Account for new writes/content after cutover; a backup alone does not establish recovery.

## Readiness and unresolved prerequisites

Tie readiness to actual transformation, consumer, and recovery evidence. Leave unsupported stages unready and name the next required check. Do not infer a database, service, maintenance window, or universal rollback strategy.
